409 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (Natural fibres are fibres that are produced by geological processes, or1 from the bodies of plants or animals. They can be used as a component of composite materials, where the orientation of fibres impacts the properties. Natural fibres can also be matted into sheets to make paper or felt. Basically, two different types of fibre reinforcement, i.e., naturally occurred and man- made fibre. Glass, carbon and aramid are man-made fibres, also known as synthetic fibre. These fibres have better mechanical properties but they are expensive and not environmentally friendly. Natural fibres are available naturally in abundance quantity. They are obtained from plant, animal and rock, Jute, flax, kenaf, hemp, sisal, bamboo and aloe Vera are common example of plant fibres. These fibres are extracted from different parts of plant like stem, leaf, and seed. The extraction of fibres involves a number of chemical and physical processes. Cellulose, hemicellulose, lignin and pectin are the major components of the plant fibre. Among all the four components, cellulose provides structural strength to fibre. Natural fibres are good sweat absorbents and can be found in a variety of textures. Cotton fibres made from the cotton plant, for example, produce fabrics that are light in weight, soft in texture, and which can be made in various sizes and colours. Clothes made of natural fibres such as cotton are often preferred over clothing made of synthetic fibres by people living in hot and humid climates. )
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [4 5]
>>
endobj
